csv:     105mm-m1-casualties.csv
source:  ../source.pdf p.89 = report page 75 (LEFT table of the pair)
anchor:  TABLE 48
anchor:  CASUALTIES
anchor:  INITIAL FRAGMENT VELOCITY 3,500
anchor:  # 105-MM H.E. SHELL,'Ml

# VERIFIED AGAINST THE PAGE IMAGE, 2026-08-02. All 55 cells were read off a
# 200-dpi render of source.pdf p.89 and match this CSV exactly; the caption
# "TABLE 48 / CASUALTIES" is printed above this table in the left half of the
# page. See 75mm-m48-casualties.invariant for the shared provenance note.
#
# See 75mm-m48-casualties.invariant for the column definitions. The 58 ft-lb
# energy closure below was the only column-identity discriminator available
# while these tables were transcribed off the flattened markdown; with the scan
# retained, checks/ordnance-1944-page-geometry.py now checks identity directly
# from where the values are printed on the page.
#
# CROSS-SHELL CLOSURE: this shell and the 155 mm M107 share INITIAL FRAGMENT
# VELOCITY 3,500 F/S, and (m, v) is a single-fragment ballistics result
# independent of shell size -- so the two tables, typeset four pages apart,
# should print the same (m, v) at the same range. They do on 8 of the 10 shared
# ranges. They differ in the last digit at r = 300 ft (0.166/598 here against
# 0.161/598) and r = 400 ft (0.232/507 against 0.233/505). Both readings were
# re-checked at 500 dpi and both are faithful: the divergence is the SOURCE's.
# The 155 mm r=300 row is the weakest energy closure in the whole six-table set
# at 55.9 ft-lb (-3.6%); this one gives 57.7 ft-lb.
row:        0.5 * (m_oz / 16 / 32.174) * v_fps**2 == 58 within 5%

monotonic:  r_ft increasing
monotonic:  N non-increasing
monotonic:  B non-increasing
monotonic:  m_oz non-decreasing
monotonic:  v_fps non-increasing
